PathwayData.put("data/grammar_vocabulary/units/L10U3.json",{"strand":"grammar_vocabulary","level":10,"cefr":"B2+","unit":3,"theme":"Cities and Communities","essential_question":"What makes a city a good place to live?","lessons":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1","title":"Millions of Residents, Most of Whom Drive: Quantifier + Relative Clauses","objectives":["Students will be able to add information about part of a group with quantifier + of whom / of which.","Students will be able to avoid comma splices by using \"of whom / of which\" instead of \"of them / of it\".","I can write \"The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.\"","I can write \"The metro has six lines, all of which connect busy districts.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من إضافة معلومات عن جزء من مجموعة بالتركيب كمية + of whom / of which.","سيتمكن الطلاب من تجنب ربط الجمل بالفاصلة باستخدام of whom / of which بدلًا من of them / of it.","أستطيع أن أكتب The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.","أستطيع أن أكتب The metro has six lines, all of which connect busy districts."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"infrastructure","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnfrəˈstrʌktʃɚ/","definition":"the basic systems a city needs, such as roads, water, power and transportation","arabic":"البنية التحتية","example":"The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W01"},{"word":"urban","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɝbən/","definition":"connected with towns and cities","arabic":"حضري","example":"Urban areas often have problems with traffic.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W02"},{"word":"density","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɛnsəti/","definition":"the number of people or buildings in a particular area","arabic":"كثافة","example":"Population density is highest in the city center.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W03"},{"word":"congestion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈdʒɛstʃən/","definition":"a situation in which roads are too full of traffic","arabic":"ازدحام","example":"Traffic congestion wastes millions of hours every year.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"To add information about part of a group, use a quantifier + of whom (people) or of which (things) in a non-defining relative clause, after a comma: The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars. The metro has six lines, all of which link busy districts.\nQuantifiers: all, most, many, much, some, a few, few, none, both, neither, either, each, half, one, two, several, the majority, a number. Also: the + superlative + of which / of whom (the oldest of which), and noun + of which (the old fort, the walls of which are made of mud brick = whose walls).\nDon't use them / it here – it creates a comma splice: (not: …millions of residents, most of them depend on cars.) ✔ …most of whom depend on cars. (Or make two sentences.) Don't use who after of: (not: most of who) → most of whom. The verb agrees with the quantifier: each of which makes; all of which link; neither of which has.\nquantifier + of whom (people) • quantifier + of which (things)\n, most of whom… • , all of which… • , the oldest of which… • , the walls of which…\nNOT \"most of them\" after a comma (comma splice) • NOT \"of who\"\nagreement: each / neither of which + singular • all / both of which + plural","examples":["The city has three new parks, all of which are free.","We interviewed twenty commuters, few of whom were happy with the traffic.","The district has two old markets, neither of which has parking.","The project employed hundreds of workers, many of whom came from nearby towns.","The towers, the tallest of which has sixty floors, dominate the skyline.","The old fort, the walls of which are made of mud brick, is now a museum.","The council received 500 suggestions, half of which were about parking.","I have two cousins in Jeddah, both of whom work in the historic district."],"common_errors":["✗ The city has millions of residents, most of them depend on cars. → ✓ The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars. (Avoid a comma splice.)","✗ I spoke to the architects, some of who designed the library. → ✓ I spoke to the architects, some of whom designed the library. (of whom.)","✗ The city has two bridges, both of whom were built in the 1980s. → ✓ The city has two bridges, both of which were built in the 1980s. (Things → of which.)","✗ The parks, each of which have a playground, are very popular. → ✓ The parks, each of which has a playground, are very popular. (each → singular.)"],"arabic":"لإضافة معلومة عن جزء من مجموعة نستخدم كمية + of whom للأشخاص أو of which للأشياء بعد فاصلة: millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.\nالكميات: all وmost وmany وsome وfew وnone وboth وneither وeach وhalf وthe majority، ومعها the oldest of which وthe walls of which.\nلا نستخدم most of them بعد الفاصلة لأنها تصنع ربطًا خاطئًا بالفاصلة (comma splice)، ولا نقول of who بل of whom.\nالفعل يتفق مع الكمية: each of which makes، all of which link."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Millions of Residents, Most of Whom Drive: Quantifier + Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U3-LS1 | Millions of Residents, Most of Whom Drive: Quantifier + Relative Clauses","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Listen to the start of “A City for People”: In just a few decades, Riyadh has grown from a modest town into a capital of millions of people, most of whom depend on cars to get around. For years, new neighborhoods spread across the desert, many of which had no parks, sidewalks or public transportation. Look at the green words.","on_screen":"In just a few decades, Riyadh has grown from a modest town into a capital of millions of people, most of whom depend on cars to get around. For years, new neighborhoods spread across the desert, many of which had no parks, sidewalks or public transportation.","visual":"Image GV-L10-U3-LS1-IMG01 animates gently; target words glow Leaf Green as they are spoken."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"To add information about part of a group, use a quantifier + of whom (people) or of which (things) in a non-defining relative clause, after a comma: The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars. The metro has six lines, all of which link busy districts. Quantifiers: all, most, many, much, some, a few, few, none, both, neither, either, each, half, one, two, several, the majority, a number. Also: the + superlative + of which / of whom (the oldest of which), and noun + of which (the old fort, the walls of which are made of mud brick = whose walls).","on_screen":"quantifier + of whom (people) • quantifier + of which (things) • , most of whom… • , all of which… • , the oldest of which… • , the walls of which… • NOT \"most of them\" after a comma (comma splice) • NOT \"of who\"","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The city has three new parks, all of which are free. We interviewed twenty commuters, few of whom were happy with the traffic. The district has two old markets, neither of which has parking.","on_screen":"The city has three new parks, all of which are free. / We interviewed twenty commuters, few of whom were happy with the traffic. / The district has two old markets, neither of which has parking.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The city has millions of residents, most of them depend on cars.” That’s not right. We say: “The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.” Avoid a comma splice.","on_screen":"✗ The city has millions of residents, most of them depend on cars.   ✓ The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Most of whom\" – people or things? … Think! … People. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","لإضافة معلومة عن جزء من مجموعة نستخدم كمية + of whom للأشخاص أو of which للأشياء بعد فاصلة: millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The city has millions of residents, most of them depend on cars. — الصحيح: The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"A City for People","body":"In just a few decades, Riyadh has grown from a modest town into a capital of millions of people, most of whom depend on cars to get around. For years, new neighborhoods spread across the desert, many of which had no parks, sidewalks or public transportation. The result was urban sprawl, long commutes and heavy traffic congestion.\nToday, planners are trying to change this. The Riyadh Metro, which opened in stages from late 2024, has six lines, all of which link busy districts with the city center. New bus routes connect the stations to residential areas, some of which were difficult to reach without a car.\nOther cities are changing too. In Jeddah, the historic district of Al-Balad, much of which had fallen into disrepair, is being carefully restored. Its coral-stone houses, the oldest of which are several centuries old, attract visitors from around the world.\nPlanners have also learned to listen to residents, few of whom were asked for their opinion in the past. Good neighborhoods, they now argue, need shaded walkways, playgrounds, clinics and local shops, each of which makes daily life easier and encourages people to walk.\nNot everyone agrees on the best approach. Two big questions remain, neither of which has an easy answer: How can a city grow without losing its heritage? And how can it stay affordable for families, the majority of whom simply want a safe, pleasant place to live?","word_count":237,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"Riyadh has millions of residents, most of ___ depend on cars.","options":["whom","who","which","them"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"quantifier + of whom (people).","explanation_ar":"of whom للأشخاص."},{"q":"New neighborhoods spread across the desert, many of ___ had no parks.","options":["them","which","that","whom"],"answer":"which","explanation":"quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"of which للأشياء."},{"q":"The metro has six lines, all of which ___ busy districts with the city center.","options":["links","linking","link","is linking"],"answer":"link","explanation":"Agreement after each / all / neither of which.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"Al-Balad, much of ___ had fallen into disrepair, is being restored.","options":["it","whom","which","what"],"answer":"which","explanation":"quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"of which للأشياء."},{"q":"Its coral-stone houses, the oldest of ___ are centuries old, attract visitors.","options":["whom","that","them","which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"the + superlative + of which / of whom.","explanation_ar":"the oldest of which."},{"q":"The planning committee interviewed forty residents, ______ said that they wanted more shaded walkways near their homes.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["most of which","most of whom","most of them","most of who"],"answer":"most of whom","explanation":"of whom / of which, not of them (comma splice).","explanation_ar":"تجنب of them."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Join the sentences with the words in brackets.","items":[{"q":"The city built three new parks.  +  All of them are open to the public. (all of which)","options":[],"answer":"The city built three new parks, all of which are open to the public.","explanation":"quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"of which للأشياء."},{"q":"We interviewed twenty commuters.  +  Few of them were happy with the traffic. (few of whom)","options":[],"answer":"We interviewed twenty commuters, few of whom were happy with the traffic.","explanation":"quantifier + of whom (people).","explanation_ar":"of whom للأشخاص."},{"q":"The district has two old markets.  +  Neither of them has parking. (neither of which)","options":[],"answer":"The district has two old markets, neither of which has parking.","explanation":"Agreement after each / all / neither of which.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"The planners met the residents, most of them wanted a new park.","options":[],"answer":"The planners met the residents, most of whom wanted a new park.","explanation":"most of them → most of whom. of whom / of which, not of them (comma splice).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: most of them → most of whom. تجنب of them."},{"q":"The city has two bridges, both of whom were built in the 1980s.","options":[],"answer":"The city has two bridges, both of which were built in the 1980s.","explanation":"both of whom → both of which. quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: both of whom → both of which. of which للأشياء."},{"q":"I spoke to the architects, some of who designed the new library.","options":[],"answer":"I spoke to the architects, some of whom designed the new library.","explanation":"some of who → some of whom. quantifier + of whom (people).","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: some of who → some of whom. of whom للأشخاص."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Describe your city.","items":[{"q":"Write about the schools in your area with \"…, some of which…\".","options":[],"answer":"There are five schools in my area, some of which have football fields.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about people you know with \"…, both / all of whom…\".","options":[],"answer":"I have three uncles, all of whom live in Taif.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about buildings with \"…, the oldest / tallest of which…\".","options":[],"answer":"Our city has many mosques, the oldest of which is over a century old.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about streets with \"…, none of which…\".","options":[],"answer":"Our neighborhood has four streets, none of which has a bike lane.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write about a building with \"…, the + noun + of which…\".","options":[],"answer":"The old school, the windows of which are made of colored glass, is now a library.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A city profile.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 sentences about your city's population or residents with \"of whom\".","options":[],"answer":"Taif has many students, most of whom attend local schools. The city welcomes thousands of summer visitors, many of whom come from Jeddah and Makkah.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences about your city's places with \"of which\".","options":[],"answer":"Taif has several rose farms, some of which are open to visitors. There are three big parks, all of which are busy in summer.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about a problem in your city using \"…, neither / none of which…\".","options":[],"answer":"There are two main roads to the city center, neither of which has enough parking.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Grammar Hot Seat","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team sits in the hot seat facing the class.","The teacher shows a cue card behind the student; the team gives a clue using non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which without saying the word.","The student in the hot seat guesses the cue word within 30 seconds.","Teams score 1 point for a correct guess and 1 point for a correct clue sentence. Rotate seats."],"data":[{"label":"most of whom"},{"label":"all of which"},{"label":"some of which"},{"label":"neither of which"},{"label":"the oldest of which"},{"label":"whoever"},{"label":"wherever"},{"label":"however + adjective"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The neighborhood has five schools, two of ___ were built last year.","options":["them","which","whom","that"],"answer":"which","explanation":"quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"of which للأشياء."},{"q":"The project employed hundreds of workers, many of ___ came from nearby towns.","options":["them","which","who","whom"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"quantifier + of whom (people).","explanation_ar":"of whom للأشخاص."},{"q":"The city opened four libraries, each of which ___ a children's section.","options":["having","are having","has","have"],"answer":"has","explanation":"Agreement after each / all / neither of which.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"","options":["The bus routes, most of who pass the metro stations, are very convenient.","The bus routes, most of them pass the metro stations, are very convenient.","The bus routes, most of which pass the metro stations, are very convenient.","The bus routes, most of that pass the metro stations, are very convenient."],"answer":"The bus routes, most of which pass the metro stations, are very convenient.","explanation":"quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"of which للأشياء."},{"q":"","options":["We surveyed the shop owners, half of whom supported the plan.","We surveyed the shop owners half of whom supported the plan.","We surveyed the shop owners, half of them supported the plan.","We surveyed the shop owners, half of who supported the plan."],"answer":"We surveyed the shop owners, half of whom supported the plan.","explanation":"of whom / of which, not of them (comma splice).","explanation_ar":"تجنب of them."},{"q":"The towers, the tallest of ___ has sixty floors, dominate the skyline.","options":["which","whom","them","it"],"answer":"which","explanation":"the + superlative + of which / of whom.","explanation_ar":"the oldest of which."},{"q":"The old fort, the walls ___ are made of mud brick, is now a museum.","options":["that of","of whom","which","of which"],"answer":"of which","explanation":"noun + of which (= whose).","explanation_ar":"the walls of which."},{"q":"In the 1970s and 1980s, the city built dozens of new suburbs, ______ were designed entirely around the car.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["many of them","many of which","many which","many of whom"],"answer":"many of which","explanation":"of whom / of which, not of them (comma splice).","explanation_ar":"تجنب of them."},{"q":"The survey included 2,000 young residents, ______ said that they would use public transportation if it were faster than driving.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["the majority whom","the majority of whom","the majority of which","the majority of them"],"answer":"the majority of whom","explanation":"quantifier + of whom (people).","explanation_ar":"of whom للأشخاص."},{"q":"In \"…two questions, neither of which has an easy answer,\" why is \"has\" singular?","options":["\"neither\" means not one and not the other","\"questions\" is singular","it is in the past","\"which\" is always singular"],"answer":"\"neither\" means not one and not the other","explanation":"Agreement after each / all / neither of which.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-IMG01","prompt":"A split image of a Saudi city: on the left, a crowded highway at rush hour; on the right, a modern metro station with passengers, a shaded pedestrian street and a restored coral-stone house in a historic district. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A split image of a Saudi city: on the left, a crowded highway at rush hour; on the right, a modern metro station with passengers, a shaded pedestrian street and a restored coral-stone house in a historic district"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-IMG02","prompt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which, with Zaki the Falcon pointing at a blank board on the right; leave large empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"clean background panel for a grammar box about non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things)"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows infrastructure (the basic systems a city needs, such as roads, water, power and transportation); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing infrastructure: the basic systems a city needs, such as roads, water, power and transportation"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows urban (connected with towns and cities); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing urban: connected with towns and cities"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows density (the number of people or buildings in a particular area); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing density: the number of people or buildings in a particular area"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows congestion (a situation in which roads are too full of traffic); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing congestion: a situation in which roads are too full of traffic"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS1-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Grammar Hot Seat”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Grammar Hot Seat"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W01","script":"infrastructure. infrastructure. The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W02","script":"urban. urban. Urban areas often have problems with traffic.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W03","script":"density. density. Population density is highest in the city center.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-W04","script":"congestion. congestion. Traffic congestion wastes millions of hours every year.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-T1","script":"In just a few decades, Riyadh has grown from a modest town into a capital of millions of people, most of whom depend on cars to get around. For years, new neighborhoods spread across the desert, many of which had no parks, sidewalks or public transportation. The result was urban sprawl, long commutes and heavy traffic congestion. Today, planners are trying to change this. The Riyadh Metro, which opened in stages from late 2024, has six lines, all of which link busy districts with the city center. New bus routes connect the stations to residential areas, some of which were difficult to reach without a car. Other cities are changing too. In Jeddah, the historic district of Al-Balad, much of which had fallen into disrepair, is being carefully restored. Its coral-stone houses, the oldest of which are several centuries old, attract visitors from around the world. Planners have also learned to listen to residents, few of whom were asked for their opinion in the past. Good neighborhoods, they now argue, need shaded walkways, playgrounds, clinics and local shops, each of which makes daily life easier and encourages people to walk. Not everyone agrees on the best approach. Two big questions remain, neither of which has an easy answer: How can a city grow without losing its heritage? And how can it stay affordable for families, the majority of whom simply want a safe, pleasant place to live?","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS1-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Millions of Residents, Most of Whom Drive: Quantifier + Relative Clauses”. Let’s learn non-defining relative clauses with quantifiers: all / most / some / many / few / none / both / neither / each / half / the majority + of whom (people) / of which (things); the + superlative + of which (the oldest of which); noun + of which (the walls of which); avoiding comma splices (most of them); agreement after each of which / all of which together. Listen to the start of “A City for People”: In just a few decades, Riyadh has grown from a modest town into a capital of millions of people, most of whom depend on cars to get around. For years, new neighborhoods spread across the desert, many of which had no parks, sidewalks or public transportation. Look at the green words. To add information about part of a group, use a quantifier + of whom (people) or of which (things) in a non-defining relative clause, after a comma: The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars. The metro has six lines, all of which link busy districts. Quantifiers: all, most, many, much, some, a few, few, none, both, neither, either, each, half, one, two, several, the majority, a number. Also: the + superlative + of which / of whom (the oldest of which), and noun + of which (the old fort, the walls of which are made of mud brick = whose walls). Here are some examples. The city has three new parks, all of which are free. We interviewed twenty commuters, few of whom were happy with the traffic. The district has two old markets, neither of which has parking. Be careful! Some learners say: “The city has millions of residents, most of them depend on cars.” That’s not right. We say: “The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.” Avoid a comma splice. Quick check: \"Most of whom\" – people or things? … Think! … People. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The city has millions of residents, most of whom depend on cars.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2","title":"Whoever Designed This Park Understood People: whoever, whatever, wherever, however","objectives":["Students will be able to use whoever, whatever, whichever, wherever and whenever to refer to any person, thing, place or time.","Students will be able to use however + adjective and no matter + question word for concession.","I can write \"Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces.\"","I can write \"However busy the streets are, the metro is always on time.\""],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام whoever وwhatever وwhichever وwherever وwhenever للإشارة إلى أي شخص أو شيء أو مكان أو زمن.","سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام however + صفة وno matter + أداة استفهام للتعبير عن التنازل.","أستطيع أن أكتب Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces.","أستطيع أن أكتب However busy the streets are, the metro is always on time."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"sustainable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/səˈsteɪnəbəl/","definition":"able to continue for a long time without harming the environment","arabic":"مستدام","example":"The city needs sustainable transportation.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W01"},{"word":"pedestrian","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈdɛstriən/","definition":"a person who is walking, not driving","arabic":"المشاة / الراجل","example":"The street is now closed to cars and open only to pedestrians.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W02"},{"word":"residential","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌrɛzɪˈdɛnʃəl/","definition":"used for homes, not offices or factories","arabic":"سكني","example":"They live in a quiet residential area.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W03"},{"word":"commute","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kəˈmjut/","definition":"to travel regularly between home and work or school","arabic":"يتنقّل يوميًا","example":"Many people commute more than an hour each way.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W04"}],"explanation":{"text":"-ever words mean any… / it doesn't matter which…. Whoever = any person who: Whoever designed this park understood people. Whatever = anything that: The council will consider whatever residents suggest. Whichever = any one (from a limited choice): Take whichever route is faster. Wherever = any place where / every place; whenever = any time / every time: Whenever it rains, the underpass floods.\n-ever clauses also show concession (= it doesn't matter): Wherever you go in Al-Balad, you see history. Whatever happens, the mosque will be protected. However + adjective/adverb + subject + verb: However busy the streets are, the metro is on time. The same idea: No matter who / what / where / when / how: No matter how busy the streets are, …\nWhoever or whomever? Choose the form the -ever clause needs: if it is the subject of its own verb, use whoever (The council will listen to whoever attends). In formal English, use whomever only as an object (Invite whomever you like). Word order after however: (not: However the traffic is heavy) → However heavy the traffic is.\nwhoever = any person who • whatever = anything that • whichever = any one of a choice\nwherever = any place • whenever = any time\nHowever + adjective / adverb + subject + verb (= no matter how)\nwhoever + verb (subject) • whomever = object (formal)","examples":["Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces.","You can take whichever bus comes first; both stop at the station.","Wherever you go in the old city, you can see coral-stone houses.","Whenever I visit Taif, I buy roses.","However busy the streets are, the metro is always on time.","Whatever happens, the historic mosque will be protected.","No matter where you live, you should have a park nearby.","The council will listen to whoever attends the meeting."],"common_errors":["✗ Whatever wins the competition will design the square. → ✓ Whoever wins the competition will design the square. (A person → whoever.)","✗ However the traffic is heavy, the bus lane stays clear. → ✓ However heavy the traffic is, the bus lane stays clear. (However + adjective + subject + verb.)","✗ The council will listen to whomever attends. → ✓ The council will listen to whoever attends. (Subject of attends → whoever.)","✗ No matter how the city is big, it needs parks. → ✓ No matter how big the city is, it needs parks. (how + adjective + subject + verb.)"],"arabic":"الكلمات المنتهية بـ -ever تعني «أيّ… / لا يهم»: whoever (أيّ شخص) وwhatever (أيّ شيء) وwhichever (أيّ واحد من اختيارات محددة) وwherever (أيّ مكان) وwhenever (أيّ وقت).\nتُستخدم أيضًا للتنازل: Wherever you go in Al-Balad, you see history، وWhatever happens, the mosque will be protected.\nHowever + صفة + فاعل + فعل = no matter how: However busy the streets are, the metro is on time.\nwhoever عندما تكون فاعلًا لفعلها: listen to whoever attends، وwhomever مفعول به في الأسلوب الرسمي."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Whoever Designed This Park Understood People: whoever, whatever, wherever, however”. Let’s learn -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U3-LS2 | Whoever Designed This Park Understood People: whoever, whatever, wherever, however","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Remember these patterns: Whoever wins the competition will design the new square. No matter what they say, I won't change my mind. (In questions, -ever expresses surprise: Whatever happened to the old cinema? However did you find this place?)","on_screen":"Whoever wins the competition will design the new square. | No matter what they say, I won't change my mind. | (In questions, -ever expresses surprise: Whatever happened to the old cinema? However did you find this place?)","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"-ever words mean any… / it doesn't matter which…. Whoever = any person who: Whoever designed this park understood people. Whatever = anything that: The council will consider whatever residents suggest. Whichever = any one (from a limited choice): Take whichever route is faster. Wherever = any place where / every place; whenever = any time / every time: Whenever it rains, the underpass floods. -ever clauses also show concession (= it doesn't matter): Wherever you go in Al-Balad, you see history. Whatever happens, the mosque will be protected. However + adjective/adverb + subject + verb: However busy the streets are, the metro is on time. The same idea: No matter who / what / where / when / how: No matter how busy the streets are, …","on_screen":"whoever = any person who • whatever = anything that • whichever = any one of a choice • wherever = any place • whenever = any time • However + adjective / adverb + subject + verb (= no matter how)","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces. You can take whichever bus comes first; both stop at the station. Wherever you go in the old city, you can see coral-stone houses.","on_screen":"Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces. / You can take whichever bus comes first; both stop at the station. / Wherever you go in the old city, you can see coral-stone houses.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “Whatever wins the competition will design the square.” That’s not right. We say: “Whoever wins the competition will design the square.” A person → whoever.","on_screen":"✗ Whatever wins the competition will design the square.   ✓ Whoever wins the competition will design the square.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"Whoever designed this park…\" – do we know who designed it? … Think! … No – it means \"the person who, whoever it was\". Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces.\"","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces.\"","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الكلمات المنتهية بـ -ever تعني «أيّ… / لا يهم»: whoever (أيّ شخص) وwhatever (أيّ شيء) وwhichever (أيّ واحد من اختيارات محددة) وwherever (أيّ مكان) وwhenever (أيّ وقت).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: Whatever wins the competition will design the square. — الصحيح: Whoever wins the competition will design the square.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"___ designed this park understood how people use public spaces.","options":["Whatever","Whichever","Whoever","Wherever"],"answer":"Whoever","explanation":"whoever = any person who.","explanation_ar":"whoever."},{"q":"You can take ___ route you prefer; both go to the station.","options":["wherever","whichever","whoever","however"],"answer":"whichever","explanation":"whichever = any one of a limited choice.","explanation_ar":"whichever."},{"q":"___ you go in the old city, you can see coral-stone houses.","options":["Whichever","Whoever","Wherever","Whatever"],"answer":"Wherever","explanation":"wherever / whenever = any place / any time.","explanation_ar":"wherever / whenever."},{"q":"___ busy the streets are, the metro is always on time.","options":["However","Whenever","Whatever","Whichever"],"answer":"However","explanation":"However + adjective + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"However + صفة."},{"q":"The council will consider ___ residents suggest at the meeting.","options":["whatever","however","wherever","whoever"],"answer":"whatever","explanation":"whatever = anything that.","explanation_ar":"whatever."},{"q":"The new transport card is designed to be flexible: passengers can use it ______ they travel in the city, whether by metro, bus or bike.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["whoever","however","whatever","wherever"],"answer":"wherever","explanation":"wherever / whenever = any place / any time.","explanation_ar":"wherever / whenever."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-B1","type":"gap_fill","instructions":"Guided practice: Complete with an -ever word or \"no matter\". Word bank: Whenever, whoever, No matter how","items":[{"q":"______ (any time) it rains heavily, the underpass floods.","options":[],"answer":"Whenever","explanation":"wherever / whenever = any place / any time.","explanation_ar":"wherever / whenever."},{"q":"The prize will go to ______ (the person who) designs the best playground.","options":[],"answer":"whoever","explanation":"whoever = any person who.","explanation_ar":"whoever."},{"q":"______ (= however) hard it is, we must protect our heritage.","options":[],"answer":"No matter how","explanation":"no matter who / what / where / how.","explanation_ar":"no matter."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Correct the mistake.","items":[{"q":"Whatever wins the competition will design the new square.","options":[],"answer":"Whoever wins the competition will design the new square.","explanation":"Whatever → Whoever. whoever = any person who.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: Whatever → Whoever. whoever."},{"q":"However the traffic is heavy, the bus lane stays clear.","options":[],"answer":"However heavy the traffic is, the bus lane stays clear.","explanation":"However the traffic is heavy → However heavy the traffic is. However + adjective + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: However the traffic is heavy → However heavy the traffic is. However + صفة."},{"q":"You can sit whoever you like in the garden.","options":[],"answer":"You can sit wherever you like in the garden.","explanation":"whoever → wherever. wherever / whenever = any place / any time.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: whoever → wherever. wherever / whenever."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Write about your city.","items":[{"q":"Begin with \"Whoever…\" about a place you like.","options":[],"answer":"Whoever planned the corniche in Jeddah made a wonderful place for families.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"Whenever I…\".","options":[],"answer":"Whenever I walk through the old market, I smell spices and perfume.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"However + adjective…\".","options":[],"answer":"However hot the weather is, the park is full in the evening.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Begin with \"No matter where…\".","options":[],"answer":"No matter where you live in the city, you should be able to reach a clinic easily.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a welcome sign with \"Whoever you are…\".","options":[],"answer":"Whoever you are and wherever you come from, you are welcome in our library.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Rules for a public park.","items":[{"q":"Write 2 park rules with -ever words.","options":[],"answer":"Whoever uses the barbecue area must clean it afterward. Visitors may sit wherever they like, except on the flower beds.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"Whatever happens,…\".","options":[],"answer":"Whatever happens, the old trees in the park will be protected.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence with \"However + adverb…\".","options":[],"answer":"However early you arrive, you will find people walking around the lake.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Sentence Builder Race","mechanic":"sentence_builder","rules":["Each team receives the same tile set.","In 5 minutes, build as many different correct sentences as possible using -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal).","Write each sentence on the team sheet.","Swap sheets with another team to check. One point per correct sentence; no points for repeats."],"data":[{"cue":"millions of residents / most / depend on cars (most of whom)"},{"cue":"six lines / all / connect districts (all of which)"},{"cue":"many neighborhoods / some / have no parks (some of which)"},{"cue":"two markets / neither / have parking (neither of which)"},{"cue":"coral houses / the oldest / be centuries old"},{"cue":"the person who / design the park / understand people (Whoever)"},{"cue":"any place / you / go / in Al-Balad / see history (Wherever)"},{"cue":"the traffic / be / very busy / the bus lane / stay clear (However busy)"},{"cue":"anything / happen / protect the mosque (Whatever)"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"___ visits Taif in summer enjoys its cool weather.","options":["However","Whatever","Wherever","Whoever"],"answer":"Whoever","explanation":"whoever = any person who.","explanation_ar":"whoever."},{"q":"There are two parking areas; you can use ___ is closer to the entrance.","options":["whichever","however","wherever","whoever"],"answer":"whichever","explanation":"whichever = any one of a limited choice.","explanation_ar":"whichever."},{"q":"___ I walk through Al-Balad, I discover something new.","options":["Whichever","Whatever","Whoever","Whenever"],"answer":"Whenever","explanation":"wherever / whenever = any place / any time.","explanation_ar":"wherever / whenever."},{"q":"___ happens, the historic mosque will be protected.","options":["Wherever","Whatever","Whoever","Whichever"],"answer":"Whatever","explanation":"whatever = anything that.","explanation_ar":"whatever."},{"q":"","options":["Whatever expensive the project is, it will improve our city.","However the project is expensive, it will improve our city.","However expensive the project is, it will improve our city.","However expensive is the project, it will improve our city."],"answer":"However expensive the project is, it will improve our city.","explanation":"However + adjective + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"However + صفة."},{"q":"","options":["The council will listen to whoever attends the meeting.","The council will listen to wherever attends the meeting.","The council will listen to whatever attends the meeting.","The council will listen to whomever attends the meeting."],"answer":"The council will listen to whoever attends the meeting.","explanation":"whoever (subject) vs whomever (object).","explanation_ar":"whoever أم whomever."},{"q":"In formal English, which word completes \"You may invite ___ you like to the opening\"?","options":["wherever","whomever","whatever","whoever's"],"answer":"whomever","explanation":"whoever (subject) vs whomever (object).","explanation_ar":"whoever أم whomever."},{"q":"\"No matter where you live\" has the same meaning as ___.","options":["However you live","Whoever you live","Wherever you live","Whatever you live"],"answer":"Wherever you live","explanation":"no matter who / what / where / how.","explanation_ar":"no matter."},{"q":"The architect believes that a good public square should welcome ______ wants to use it, from children playing football to elderly residents looking for shade.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["whoever","wherever","whomever","whatever"],"answer":"whoever","explanation":"whoever (subject) vs whomever (object).","explanation_ar":"whoever أم whomever."},{"q":"______ a city becomes, planners must find space for parks, schools and clinics within walking distance of people's homes.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Whatever large","However large is","However, large","However large"],"answer":"However large","explanation":"However + adjective + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"However + صفة."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-IMG01","prompt":"A busy public square in a Saudi city in the evening: families on benches, children playing, a shaded walkway, a signpost \"Wherever you are, you're never more than 10 minutes from a park\", and a poster \"Whoever you are, you're welcome here\". Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A busy public square in a Saudi city in the evening: families on benches, children playing, a shaded walkway, a signpost \"Wherever you are, you're never more than 10 minutes from a park\", and a poster \"Whoever you are, you're welcome here\""},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-IMG02","prompt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal): a green tick panel and a soft red cross panel with simple objects from the theme “Cities and Communities”; empty space for text overlay. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two-panel visual contrasting correct and incorrect use of -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows sustainable (able to continue for a long time without harming the environment); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing sustainable: able to continue for a long time without harming the environment"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows pedestrian (a person who is walking, not driving); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing pedestrian: a person who is walking, not driving"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows residential (used for homes, not offices or factories); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing residential: used for homes, not offices or factories"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows commute (to travel regularly between home and work or school); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing commute: to travel regularly between home and work or school"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS2-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Sentence Builder Race”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Sentence Builder Race"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W01","script":"sustainable. sustainable. The city needs sustainable transportation.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W02","script":"pedestrian. pedestrian. The street is now closed to cars and open only to pedestrians.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W03","script":"residential. residential. They live in a quiet residential area.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-W04","script":"commute. commute. Many people commute more than an hour each way.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS2-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Whoever Designed This Park Understood People: whoever, whatever, wherever, however”. Let’s learn -ever words: whoever, whatever, whichever (= any person / thing / one that) as subjects and objects; wherever, whenever (= any place / time); however + adjective / adverb (= no matter how); -ever clauses for concession (Wherever you go, …); no matter who / what / where / how; whoever vs whomever (formal) together. Remember these patterns: Whoever wins the competition will design the new square. No matter what they say, I won't change my mind. (In questions, -ever expresses surprise: Whatever happened to the old cinema? However did you find this place?) -ever words mean any… / it doesn't matter which…. Whoever = any person who: Whoever designed this park understood people. Whatever = anything that: The council will consider whatever residents suggest. Whichever = any one (from a limited choice): Take whichever route is faster. Wherever = any place where / every place; whenever = any time / every time: Whenever it rains, the underpass floods. -ever clauses also show concession (= it doesn't matter): Wherever you go in Al-Balad, you see history. Whatever happens, the mosque will be protected. However + adjective/adverb + subject + verb: However busy the streets are, the metro is on time. The same idea: No matter who / what / where / when / how: No matter how busy the streets are, … Here are some examples. Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces. You can take whichever bus comes first; both stop at the station. Wherever you go in the old city, you can see coral-stone houses. Be careful! Some learners say: “Whatever wins the competition will design the square.” That’s not right. We say: “Whoever wins the competition will design the square.” A person → whoever. Quick check: \"Whoever designed this park…\" – do we know who designed it? … Think! … No – it means \"the person who, whoever it was\". Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"Whoever designed this park understood how people use public spaces.\"","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3","title":"Urban, Urbanize, Urbanization: City Vocabulary and Word Families","objectives":["Students will be able to use urban planning vocabulary and collocations to discuss city life.","Students will be able to choose the correct form from a word family (urban → urbanize → urbanization).","I can talk about traffic congestion, urban sprawl and affordable housing.","I can build word families such as reside → resident → residential."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من استخدام مفردات التخطيط العمراني ومتلازماتها لمناقشة الحياة في المدن.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار الصيغة الصحيحة من عائلة الكلمة (urban → urbanize → urbanization).","أستطيع أن أتحدث عن الازدحام المروري والتمدد العمراني والإسكان الميسّر.","أستطيع أن أبني عائلات كلمات مثل reside → resident → residential."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"zoning","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈzoʊnɪŋ/","definition":"rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used","arabic":"تقسيم المناطق / التنظيم العمراني","example":"Zoning laws keep factories away from homes.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W01","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W01"},{"word":"renovate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈrɛnəˌveɪt/","definition":"to repair an old building and make it look new","arabic":"يرمّم / يجدّد","example":"The council renovated the old market.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W02","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W02"},{"word":"heritage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛrətədʒ/","definition":"buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past","arabic":"تراث","example":"Al-Balad is part of Jeddah's heritage.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W03","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W03"},{"word":"amenity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈmɛnəti/","definition":"a useful facility such as a park, shop, clinic or library","arabic":"مرفق / خدمة","example":"The neighborhood has good amenities.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W04","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W04"},{"word":"sprawl","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sprɔl/","definition":"the spread of a city into the land around it in an unplanned way","arabic":"التمدّد العمراني العشوائي","example":"Urban sprawl makes people depend on cars.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W05","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W05"},{"word":"inclusive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈklusɪv/","definition":"welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","arabic":"شامل / دامج","example":"Parks should be inclusive spaces for everyone.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W06","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W06"},{"word":"municipality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌmjunɪsəˈpæləti/","definition":"a city or town with its own local government","arabic":"بلدية","example":"The municipality is building new sidewalks.","image_id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W07","audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W07"}],"explanation":{"text":"City words: infrastructure (roads, water, power, transport), density (people per area), congestion (too much traffic), sprawl (unplanned spreading of a city), zoning (rules for land use), amenity (a useful facility), heritage (what the past leaves us), municipality (a city with local government), pedestrian (a person walking), residential (for homes), inclusive (for everyone), sustainable (able to last without harming the environment).\nCollocations: affordable housing; public transportation; green space; traffic congestion; population density; urban renewal / sprawl; mixed-use development (homes, shops and offices together); historic district; city council; rush hour; commute to work.\nWord families: urban → urbanize (v) → urbanization (n) → suburban (adj); reside (v) → resident (n) → residence (n) → residential (adj); congest → congestion → congested; sustain → sustainable → sustainability; include → inclusion → inclusive; dense → density → densely; pedestrian → pedestrianize; municipality → municipal. Suffixes: -ize (verb), -ization / -ity / -ion (noun), -ive / -ial / -al (adjective).\nWord families: urban: urbanize (v), urbanization (n), suburban (adj) | reside: resident (n), residence (n), residential (adj) | congest: congestion (n), congested (adj) | sustain: sustainable (adj), sustainability (n), sustainably (adv) | include: inclusion (n), inclusive (adj), inclusively (adv) | dense: density (n), densely (adv)\nCollocations/phrases: affordable housing • public transportation • green space • mixed-use development | traffic congestion • population density • urban sprawl • urban renewal | historic district • city council • rush hour • commute to work | densely populated • pedestrian zone • residential area • inclusive design","examples":["The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure.","Traffic congestion adds an hour to many people's commutes.","Rapid urbanization moved millions of people from villages to cities.","They live in a quiet residential area near the university.","The municipality is turning the old market street into a pedestrian zone.","Urban sprawl makes people depend on their cars.","The playground has inclusive equipment for children with disabilities.","Cities need affordable housing for young families."],"common_errors":["✗ The city is very congestion at rush hour. → ✓ The city is very congested at rush hour. (Adjective → congested.)","✗ Urbanize has changed the Kingdom quickly. → ✓ Urbanization has changed the Kingdom quickly. (Noun → urbanization.)","✗ The residents area is quiet. → ✓ The residential area is quiet. (Adjective → residential.)","✗ The city has many green spaces and cheap housings. → ✓ The city has many green spaces and affordable housing. (housing is uncountable; affordable is the usual word.)"],"arabic":"كلمات المدينة: infrastructure (البنية التحتية) وdensity (الكثافة) وcongestion (الازدحام) وsprawl (التمدد العشوائي) وzoning (تقسيم المناطق) وamenity (مرفق) وheritage (تراث) وmunicipality (بلدية).\nمتلازمات: affordable housing (الإسكان الميسّر) وpublic transportation (النقل العام) وgreen space (المساحات الخضراء) وtraffic congestion (الازدحام المروري).\nعائلات الكلمات: urban → urbanize → urbanization، وreside → resident → residential، وcongestion → congested.\nاللواحق: -ize للفعل، و-ization / -ity / -ion للاسم، و-ive / -ial / -al للصفة."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Urban, Urbanize, Urbanization: City Vocabulary and Word Families”. Let’s learn urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U3-LS3 | Urban, Urbanize, Urbanization: City Vocabulary and Word Families","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Meet some new words. zoning: rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used. renovate: to repair an old building and make it look new. heritage: buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past.","on_screen":"zoning | renovate | heritage","visual":"Each word card flips over to show its picture (vocabulary image briefs) and Arabic meaning."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"City words: infrastructure (roads, water, power, transport), density (people per area), congestion (too much traffic), sprawl (unplanned spreading of a city), zoning (rules for land use), amenity (a useful facility), heritage (what the past leaves us), municipality (a city with local government), pedestrian (a person walking), residential (for homes), inclusive (for everyone), sustainable (able to last without harming the environment). Collocations: affordable housing; public transportation; green space; traffic congestion; population density; urban renewal / sprawl; mixed-use development (homes, shops and offices together); historic district; city council; rush hour; commute to work.","on_screen":"urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure. Traffic congestion adds an hour to many people's commutes. Rapid urbanization moved millions of people from villages to cities.","on_screen":"The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure. / Traffic congestion adds an hour to many people's commutes. / Rapid urbanization moved millions of people from villages to cities.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The city is very congestion at rush hour.” That’s not right. We say: “The city is very congested at rush hour.” Adjective → congested.","on_screen":"✗ The city is very congestion at rush hour.   ✓ The city is very congested at rush hour.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: Is \"congestion\" a noun or an adjective? … Think! … A noun; the adjective is *congested*. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about traffic congestion, urban sprawl and affordable housing.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can talk about traffic congestion, urban sprawl and affordable housing.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","كلمات المدينة: infrastructure (البنية التحتية) وdensity (الكثافة) وcongestion (الازدحام) وsprawl (التمدد العشوائي) وzoning (تقسيم المناطق) وamenity (مرفق) وheritage (تراث) وmunicipality (بلدية).","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The city is very congestion at rush hour. — الصحيح: The city is very congested at rush hour.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أتحدث عن الازدحام المروري والتمدد العمراني والإسكان الميسّر."]},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","items":[{"q":"Heavy traffic ___ adds an hour to many commuters' journeys.","options":["zoning","heritage","congestion","density"],"answer":"congestion","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The district is mostly ___, with houses and apartment buildings but few offices.","options":["residential","pedestrian","municipal","sustainable"],"answer":"residential","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The council plans to ___ the old market street so that only people on foot can use it.","options":["renovation","urbanize","congest","pedestrianize"],"answer":"pedestrianize","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Rapid ___ has moved millions of people from villages to cities.","options":["urbanized","urbanization","urban","urbanize"],"answer":"urbanization","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Which word means \"useful facilities such as parks, shops and clinics\"?","options":["zoning","sprawl","heritage","amenities"],"answer":"amenities","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Rather than building new neighborhoods farther and farther into the desert, planners now encourage higher ______ in the city center, where more people can live close to jobs and public transportation.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["density","heritage","congestion","sprawl"],"answer":"density","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-B1","type":"match","instructions":"Guided practice: Match the word to its meaning.","items":[{"q":"infrastructure","options":["welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","rules that say how land in each area can be used","buildings and traditions passed down from the past","basic systems such as roads, water and power","a city or town with its own local government","the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it"],"answer":"basic systems such as roads, water and power","explanation":"infrastructure → basic systems such as roads, water and power. City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"sprawl","options":["welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","rules that say how land in each area can be used","buildings and traditions passed down from the past","basic systems such as roads, water and power","a city or town with its own local government","the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it"],"answer":"the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it","explanation":"sprawl → the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it. City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"heritage","options":["welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","rules that say how land in each area can be used","buildings and traditions passed down from the past","basic systems such as roads, water and power","a city or town with its own local government","the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it"],"answer":"buildings and traditions passed down from the past","explanation":"heritage → buildings and traditions passed down from the past. City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"municipality","options":["welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","rules that say how land in each area can be used","buildings and traditions passed down from the past","basic systems such as roads, water and power","a city or town with its own local government","the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it"],"answer":"a city or town with its own local government","explanation":"municipality → a city or town with its own local government. City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"zoning","options":["welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","rules that say how land in each area can be used","buildings and traditions passed down from the past","basic systems such as roads, water and power","a city or town with its own local government","the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it"],"answer":"rules that say how land in each area can be used","explanation":"zoning → rules that say how land in each area can be used. City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"inclusive","options":["welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","rules that say how land in each area can be used","buildings and traditions passed down from the past","basic systems such as roads, water and power","a city or town with its own local government","the unplanned spread of a city into the land around it"],"answer":"welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","explanation":"inclusive → welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities. City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Talk about your city.","items":[{"q":"What is the biggest problem in your city: congestion, sprawl or something else?","options":[],"answer":"The biggest problem is traffic congestion during rush hour.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Which amenities does your neighborhood have, and which does it need?","options":[],"answer":"My neighborhood has a mosque and a supermarket, but it needs a park.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"affordable housing\".","options":[],"answer":"Young families need affordable housing near their workplaces.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"urbanization\".","options":[],"answer":"Urbanization has changed the way people live in the Kingdom.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"How can a city protect its heritage?","options":[],"answer":"A city can protect its heritage by renovating old buildings instead of demolishing them.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: A planner's report.","items":[{"q":"Write 3 sentences about your city using 3 collocations.","options":[],"answer":"Riyadh has heavy traffic congestion at rush hour. The new metro improves public transportation. The city also needs more green space.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write 2 sentences using two forms from one word family.","options":[],"answer":"Most residents of our street are students. It is a quiet residential area.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write one sentence about what makes a city sustainable.","options":[],"answer":"A sustainable city uses clean energy and encourages people to walk or use public transportation.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Word Family Tree","mechanic":"drag_and_drop","rules":["Teams take turns drawing a word card.","Place the card on the correct branch of the tree and say a sentence with it.","Correct placement and sentence = 2 points.","The team with the most points when the tree is full wins."],"data":[{"word":"urbanize","category":"verb","family":"urban"},{"word":"urbanization","category":"noun","family":"urban"},{"word":"suburban","category":"adjective","family":"urban"},{"word":"resident","category":"noun","family":"reside"},{"word":"residence","category":"noun","family":"reside"},{"word":"residential","category":"adjective","family":"reside"},{"word":"congestion","category":"noun","family":"congest"},{"word":"congested","category":"adjective","family":"congest"},{"word":"sustainable","category":"adjective","family":"sustain"},{"word":"sustainability","category":"noun","family":"sustain"},{"word":"sustainably","category":"adverb","family":"sustain"},{"word":"inclusion","category":"noun","family":"include"},{"word":"inclusive","category":"adjective","family":"include"},{"word":"inclusively","category":"adverb","family":"include"},{"word":"density","category":"noun","family":"dense"},{"word":"densely","category":"adverb","family":"dense"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"The new metro is a major investment in the city's ___.","options":["amenity","zoning","infrastructure","heritage"],"answer":"infrastructure","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Many workers ___ more than an hour each way.","options":["commute","reside","renovate","zone"],"answer":"commute","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"The ___ of the old fort took three years.","options":["renovation","renovate","renovating","renovated"],"answer":"renovation","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Cairo is one of the most ___ populated cities in Africa.","options":["dense","densely","density","denser"],"answer":"densely","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"A city that welcomes people of all ages and abilities is ___.","options":["inclusive","include","inclusion","including"],"answer":"inclusive","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"Which adjective means \"connected with towns and cities\"?","options":["coastal","nomadic","urban","rural"],"answer":"urban","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"Homes that ordinary families can pay for are called ___ housing.","options":["expensive","cheapening","luxurious","affordable"],"answer":"affordable","explanation":"City collocation.","explanation_ar":"المتلازمة."},{"q":"The roads become very ___ at rush hour.","options":["congest","congesting","congestion","congested"],"answer":"congested","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The historic district has been carefully restored, but residents worry that rising rents may force out the small family shops that give the area its unique ______.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["density","character","infrastructure","congestion"],"answer":"character","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"Because most of the city's offices are concentrated in one district, the roads leading there become severely ______ every morning.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["renovated","inclusive","congested","residential"],"answer":"congested","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-IMG01","prompt":"An urban planner's table with a large city map: colored zones labeled \"residential\", \"commercial\", \"green space\", \"heritage area\"; small models of a metro train, trees and pedestrians; a word-family tree on the wall: urban → urbanize → urbanization. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"An urban planner's table with a large city map: colored zones labeled \"residential\", \"commercial\", \"green space\", \"heritage area\"; small models of a metro train, trees and pedestrians; a word-family tree on the wall: urban → urbanize → urbanization"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-IMG02","prompt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words; empty labels. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"word-web visual: a central circle with branches to 7 small picture icons of the lesson words"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W01","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows zoning (rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing zoning: rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W02","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows renovate (to repair an old building and make it look new); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing renovate: to repair an old building and make it look new"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W03","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows heritage (buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing heritage: buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W04","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows amenity (a useful facility such as a park, shop, clinic or library); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing amenity: a useful facility such as a park, shop, clinic or library"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W05","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows sprawl (the spread of a city into the land around it in an unplanned way); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing sprawl: the spread of a city into the land around it in an unplanned way"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W06","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows inclusive (welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing inclusive: welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-W07","prompt":"Single clear picture that shows municipality (a city or town with its own local government); centred subject, simple light background. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"Picture showing municipality: a city or town with its own local government"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS3-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Word Family Tree”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Word Family Tree"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W01","script":"zoning. zoning. Zoning laws keep factories away from homes.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W02","script":"renovate. renovate. The council renovated the old market.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W03","script":"heritage. heritage. Al-Balad is part of Jeddah's heritage.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W04","script":"amenity. amenity. The neighborhood has good amenities.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W05","script":"sprawl. sprawl. Urban sprawl makes people depend on cars.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W06","script":"inclusive. inclusive. Parks should be inclusive spaces for everyone.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-W07","script":"municipality. municipality. The municipality is building new sidewalks.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS3-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “Urban, Urbanize, Urbanization: City Vocabulary and Word Families”. Let’s learn urban planning vocabulary (infrastructure, density, congestion, sprawl, zoning, amenity, heritage, municipality, pedestrian, residential, inclusive, sustainable, commute, renovate); collocations (affordable housing, public transportation, green space, traffic congestion, mixed-use development, urban renewal, population density); word families with -ize, -ization, -ity, -ive, -ial, -er together. Meet some new words. zoning: rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used. renovate: to repair an old building and make it look new. heritage: buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past. City words: infrastructure (roads, water, power, transport), density (people per area), congestion (too much traffic), sprawl (unplanned spreading of a city), zoning (rules for land use), amenity (a useful facility), heritage (what the past leaves us), municipality (a city with local government), pedestrian (a person walking), residential (for homes), inclusive (for everyone), sustainable (able to last without harming the environment). Collocations: affordable housing; public transportation; green space; traffic congestion; population density; urban renewal / sprawl; mixed-use development (homes, shops and offices together); historic district; city council; rush hour; commute to work. Here are some examples. The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure. Traffic congestion adds an hour to many people's commutes. Rapid urbanization moved millions of people from villages to cities. Be careful! Some learners say: “The city is very congestion at rush hour.” That’s not right. We say: “The city is very congested at rush hour.” Adjective → congested. Quick check: Is \"congestion\" a noun or an adjective? … Think! … A noun; the adjective is *congested*. Great job! Now you can say: I can talk about traffic congestion, urban sprawl and affordable housing.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4","title":"No Comma Before the Verb: Avoiding Unnecessary Commas (SAT)","objectives":["Students will be able to recognize and remove commas that separate essential parts of a sentence.","Students will be able to choose the correctly punctuated option in SAT-style boundary questions.","I can write \"The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts.\" with no comma before the verb.","I can write \"Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah\" with no comma after \"such as\"."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من التعرّف على الفواصل التي تفصل أجزاء الجملة الأساسية وحذفها.","سيتمكن الطلاب من اختيار الخيار الصحيح الترقيم في أسئلة الحدود بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أكتب The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts. دون فاصلة قبل الفعل.","أستطيع أن أكتب Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah دون فاصلة بعد such as."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"infrastructure","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnfrəˈstrʌktʃɚ/","definition":"the basic systems a city needs, such as roads, water, power and transportation","arabic":"البنية التحتية","example":"The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"urban","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɝbən/","definition":"connected with towns and cities","arabic":"حضري","example":"Urban areas often have problems with traffic.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"density","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɛnsəti/","definition":"the number of people or buildings in a particular area","arabic":"كثافة","example":"Population density is highest in the city center.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"congestion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈdʒɛstʃən/","definition":"a situation in which roads are too full of traffic","arabic":"ازدحام","example":"Traffic congestion wastes millions of hours every year.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"sustainable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/səˈsteɪnəbəl/","definition":"able to continue for a long time without harming the environment","arabic":"مستدام","example":"The city needs sustainable transportation.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"pedestrian","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈdɛstriən/","definition":"a person who is walking, not driving","arabic":"المشاة / الراجل","example":"The street is now closed to cars and open only to pedestrians.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"A comma should never separate the main parts of a sentence. No comma between the subject and its verb, even when the subject is long: The new metro line that runs along the main road, connects many districts. No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause: The planners argued, that wider sidewalks reduce traffic.\nNo commas around restrictive (essential) information: The residents who live near the park use it every evening. The urban planner Rakan Al-Otaibi designed the square. (the name identifies which planner). No comma after such as / including: cities such as, Riyadh and Jeddah. No comma between two verbs that share one subject: The council approved the plan, and announced a date.\nNo comma between cumulative adjectives (a beautiful old mosque – you can't say \"old and beautiful mosque\" naturally), after and / but / so when they join words or clauses (but, the plan…), or between a preposition and its object (between, the two markets). SAT tip: if a comma breaks a sentence into pieces that can't be read without it, remove it.\nsubject no comma verb • verb no comma object / that-clause\nno commas around restrictive clauses or restrictive appositives\nsuch as / including no comma • verb and verb (one subject) no comma\ncumulative adjectives • after and / but • preposition + object no comma","examples":["The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts.","The planners argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic.","Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation.","The urban planner Rakan Al-Otaibi designed the new square.","The council approved the plan and announced a start date.","Al-Balad has many beautiful old houses.","The bus stop is between the two markets.","The residents who live near the park use it every evening."],"common_errors":["✗ The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone. → ✓ The houses in the historic district are made of coral stone. (No comma between subject and verb.)","✗ The mayor said, that the metro would open soon. → ✓ The mayor said that the metro would open soon. (No comma before a that-clause.)","✗ The city needs amenities such as, parks and clinics. → ✓ The city needs amenities such as parks and clinics. (No comma after such as.)","✗ The workers repaired the road, and painted the lines. → ✓ The workers repaired the road and painted the lines. (Two verbs, one subject → no comma.)"],"arabic":"الفاصلة لا تفصل أجزاء الجملة الأساسية: لا فاصلة بين الفاعل وفعله حتى لو كان الفاعل طويلًا، ولا بين الفعل ومفعوله أو جملة that.\nلا فواصل حول المعلومات الأساسية المقيِّدة (restrictive): The residents who live near the park…، ولا بعد such as أو including.\nلا فاصلة بين فعلين لفاعل واحد، ولا بين الصفات المتراكمة (a beautiful old mosque)، ولا بعد and / but، ولا بين حرف الجر ومجروره.\nنصيحة SAT: إذا كانت الفاصلة تقطع الجملة إلى أجزاء غير مكتملة فاحذفها."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “No Comma Before the Verb: Avoiding Unnecessary Commas (SAT)”. Let’s learn unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U3-LS4 | No Comma Before the Verb: Avoiding Unnecessary Commas (SAT)","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"A comma should never separate the main parts of a sentence. No comma between the subject and its verb, even when the subject is long: The new metro line that runs along the main road, connects many districts. No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause: The planners argued, that wider sidewalks reduce traffic.","on_screen":"unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object","visual":"Pattern strips slide in from the left; the key words are highlighted in Leaf Green."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"A comma should never separate the main parts of a sentence. No comma between the subject and its verb, even when the subject is long: The new metro line that runs along the main road, connects many districts. No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause: The planners argued, that wider sidewalks reduce traffic. No commas around restrictive (essential) information: The residents who live near the park use it every evening. The urban planner Rakan Al-Otaibi designed the square. (the name identifies which planner). No comma after such as / including: cities such as, Riyadh and Jeddah. No comma between two verbs that share one subject: The council approved the plan, and announced a date.","on_screen":"subject no comma verb • verb no comma object / that-clause • no commas around restrictive clauses or restrictive appositives • such as / including no comma • verb and verb (one subject) no comma","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts. The planners argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic. Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation.","on_screen":"The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts. / The planners argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic. / Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone.” That’s not right. We say: “The houses in the historic district are made of coral stone.” No comma between subject and verb.","on_screen":"✗ The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone.   ✓ The houses in the historic district are made of coral stone.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: \"The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone.\" Why is the comma wrong? … Think! … It separates the subject from the verb. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts.\" with no comma before the verb.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can write \"The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts.\" with no comma before the verb.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object.","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","الفاصلة لا تفصل أجزاء الجملة الأساسية: لا فاصلة بين الفاعل وفعله حتى لو كان الفاعل طويلًا، ولا بين الفعل ومفعوله أو جملة that.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone. — الصحيح: The houses in the historic district are made of coral stone.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أكتب The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts. دون فاصلة قبل الفعل."]},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Our City's New Square (editing passage)","body":"The new public square in the city center, opened last week. The planners explained, that it was designed for families and pedestrians. It has amenities such as, shaded benches, fountains and a small library. The architect Rakan Al-Otaibi visited the square, and talked to residents. Many people who live nearby said that the square was a wonderful, new place to meet.","word_count":61,"audio_id":""}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correctly punctuated answer.","items":[{"q":"The new metro line that runs along King Fahd ______ dozens of offices, hospitals and universities.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["Road, connects","Road connects,","Road, connects,","Road connects"],"answer":"Road connects","explanation":"No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"","options":["The planners argued that, wider sidewalks would reduce traffic.","The planners argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic.","The planners, argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic.","The planners argued, that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic."],"answer":"The planners argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic.","explanation":"No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفعل ومفعوله."},{"q":"","options":["The council approved, the plan and announced a start date.","The council, approved the plan and announced a start date.","The council approved the plan and announced a start date.","The council approved the plan, and announced a start date."],"answer":"The council approved the plan and announced a start date.","explanation":"No comma between two verbs with one subject.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين فعلين لفاعل واحد."},{"q":"","options":["Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation.","Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah, are investing in public transportation.","Cities, such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation.","Cities such as, Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation."],"answer":"Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation.","explanation":"No comma after such as / including.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بعد such as."},{"q":"","options":["The residents who live near the park, use it every evening.","The residents who live near the park use it every evening.","The residents, who live near the park use it every evening.","The residents who live, near the park use it every evening."],"answer":"The residents who live near the park use it every evening.","explanation":"No commas around restrictive clauses or appositives.","explanation_ar":"لا فواصل حول المعلومة الأساسية."},{"q":"The urban ______ studied how people behave in public squares and used the results to design more welcoming spaces.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["planner Rakan Al-Otaibi,","planner, Rakan Al-Otaibi","planner, Rakan Al-Otaibi,","planner Rakan Al-Otaibi"],"answer":"planner Rakan Al-Otaibi","explanation":"No commas around restrictive clauses or appositives.","explanation_ar":"لا فواصل حول المعلومة الأساسية."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-B1","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Remove the unnecessary comma.","items":[{"q":"The coral-stone houses in Al-Balad, are several centuries old.","options":[],"answer":"The coral-stone houses in Al-Balad are several centuries old.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"The municipality announced, that the new park would open in spring.","options":[],"answer":"The municipality announced that the new park would open in spring.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفعل ومفعوله."},{"q":"The neighborhood needs amenities including, a clinic and a library.","options":[],"answer":"The neighborhood needs amenities including a clinic and a library.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma after such as / including.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بعد such as."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-B2","type":"open","instructions":"Guided practice: Remove the unnecessary comma.","items":[{"q":"The workers repaired the road, and painted new crossings.","options":[],"answer":"The workers repaired the road and painted new crossings.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between two verbs with one subject.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين فعلين لفاعل واحد."},{"q":"We walked past a beautiful, old mosque.","options":[],"answer":"We walked past a beautiful old mosque.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between cumulative adjectives.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). الصفات المتراكمة."},{"q":"The bus stop is located between, the two markets.","options":[],"answer":"The bus stop is located between the two markets.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma after a preposition or a conjunction.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بعد حرف الجر أو العطف."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Edit the news article.","items":[{"q":"The new public square in the city center, opened last week.","options":[],"answer":"The new public square in the city center opened last week.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"The new public square in the city center, opened last week.","options":[],"answer":"The new public square in the city center opened last week.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"The new public square in the city center, opened last week.","options":[],"answer":"The new public square in the city center opened last week.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"The new public square in the city center, opened last week.","options":[],"answer":"The new public square in the city center opened last week.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"The new public square in the city center, opened last week.","options":[],"answer":"The new public square in the city center opened last week.","explanation":", → (delete). No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"التصحيح: , → (delete). لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write correctly punctuated sentences.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence with a long subject (no comma before the verb).","options":[],"answer":"The old houses in the historic district of our city are being renovated.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with \"such as\" listing three amenities.","options":[],"answer":"Our neighborhood needs amenities such as a park, a library and a clinic.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write a sentence with one subject and two verbs (no comma).","options":[],"answer":"The volunteers cleaned the park and planted new trees.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Spelling Relay","mechanic":"word_builder","rules":["Teams line up facing the board.","The teacher says a word; the first student runs to the board and writes the first letter.","The next student adds the next letter, and so on.","The first team with the correct spelling wins the point."],"data":[{"word":"infrastructure","clue":"the basic systems a city needs, such as roads, water, power and transportation"},{"word":"congestion","clue":"a situation in which roads are too full of traffic"},{"word":"pedestrian","clue":"a person who is walking, not driving"},{"word":"residential","clue":"used for homes, not offices or factories"},{"word":"municipality","clue":"a city or town with its own local government"},{"word":"heritage","clue":"buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past"},{"word":"urban","clue":"connected with towns and cities"},{"word":"density","clue":"the number of people or buildings in a particular area"},{"word":"sustainable","clue":"able to continue for a long time without harming the environment"},{"word":"commute","clue":"to travel regularly between home and work or school"},{"word":"zoning","clue":"rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used"},{"word":"renovate","clue":"to repair an old building and make it look new"}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"","options":["The number of people, who use the metro every day has doubled.","The number of people who use the metro every day, has doubled.","The number of people who use the metro, every day has doubled.","The number of people who use the metro every day has doubled."],"answer":"The number of people who use the metro every day has doubled.","explanation":"No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"","options":["Residents, told the council that they wanted more green space.","Residents told the council, that they wanted more green space.","Residents told the council that they wanted more green space.","Residents told, the council that they wanted more green space."],"answer":"Residents told the council that they wanted more green space.","explanation":"No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفعل ومفعوله."},{"q":"","options":["The novel, that won the national prize describes life in old Jeddah.","The novel that won the national prize, describes life in old Jeddah.","The novel that won the national prize describes life in old Jeddah.","The novel that won, the national prize describes life in old Jeddah."],"answer":"The novel that won the national prize describes life in old Jeddah.","explanation":"No commas around restrictive clauses or appositives.","explanation_ar":"لا فواصل حول المعلومة الأساسية."},{"q":"","options":["The park has inclusive equipment, and offers free activities on weekends.","The park has inclusive equipment and offers free activities on weekends.","The park has, inclusive equipment and offers free activities on weekends.","The park, has inclusive equipment and offers free activities on weekends."],"answer":"The park has inclusive equipment and offers free activities on weekends.","explanation":"No comma between two verbs with one subject.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين فعلين لفاعل واحد."},{"q":"","options":["Visitors can buy local products including, honey, roses and dates.","Visitors can buy local products including honey, roses and dates.","Visitors can buy, local products including honey, roses and dates.","Visitors can buy local products, including, honey, roses and dates."],"answer":"Visitors can buy local products including honey, roses and dates.","explanation":"No comma after such as / including.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بعد such as."},{"q":"","options":["The museum is a small white building, near, the old gate.","The museum is a small white, building near the old gate.","The museum is a small white building near the old gate.","The museum is a small, white, building near the old gate."],"answer":"The museum is a small white building near the old gate.","explanation":"No comma between cumulative adjectives.","explanation_ar":"الصفات المتراكمة."},{"q":"","options":["The plan was expensive, but the council approved it.","The plan, was expensive, but the council approved it.","The plan was expensive but, the council approved it.","The plan was expensive, but, the council approved it."],"answer":"The plan was expensive, but the council approved it.","explanation":"No comma after a preposition or a conjunction.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بعد حرف الجر أو العطف."},{"q":"According to a recent survey, the residents of the city's oldest ______ more parks and fewer cars on their narrow streets.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["neighborhoods want","neighborhoods want,","neighborhoods, want,","neighborhoods, want"],"answer":"neighborhoods want","explanation":"No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"Many cities have discovered ______ pedestrian streets can increase the number of customers who visit local shops.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["that,","that",", that,",", that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفعل ومفعوله."},{"q":"The Saudi ______ spent ten years photographing the historic houses of Al-Balad before they were restored.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["photographer, Ziyad Al-Harbi,","photographer, Ziyad Al-Harbi","photographer Ziyad Al-Harbi,","photographer Ziyad Al-Harbi"],"answer":"photographer Ziyad Al-Harbi","explanation":"No commas around restrictive clauses or appositives.","explanation_ar":"لا فواصل حول المعلومة الأساسية."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-IMG01","prompt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Cities and Communities” that shows unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object in action. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"Two or three of the programme characters in a scene about “Cities and Communities” that shows unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object in action"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-IMG02","prompt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"friendly editing-desk scene: a magnifying glass over a page with highlighted punctuation marks, Zaki holding a green pen"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS4-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Spelling Relay”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Spelling Relay"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS4-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “No Comma Before the Verb: Avoiding Unnecessary Commas (SAT)”. Let’s learn unnecessary commas: between a subject (even a long one) and its verb; between a verb and its object or that-clause; before or after a restrictive clause or restrictive appositive; after such as / including; between two verbs that share one subject; between cumulative adjectives; after a coordinating conjunction; between a preposition and its object together. A comma should never separate the main parts of a sentence. No comma between the subject and its verb, even when the subject is long: The new metro line that runs along the main road, connects many districts. No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause: The planners argued, that wider sidewalks reduce traffic. A comma should never separate the main parts of a sentence. No comma between the subject and its verb, even when the subject is long: The new metro line that runs along the main road, connects many districts. No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause: The planners argued, that wider sidewalks reduce traffic. No commas around restrictive (essential) information: The residents who live near the park use it every evening. The urban planner Rakan Al-Otaibi designed the square. (the name identifies which planner). No comma after such as / including: cities such as, Riyadh and Jeddah. No comma between two verbs that share one subject: The council approved the plan, and announced a date. Here are some examples. The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts. The planners argued that wider sidewalks would reduce traffic. Cities such as Riyadh and Jeddah are investing in public transportation. Be careful! Some learners say: “The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone.” That’s not right. We say: “The houses in the historic district are made of coral stone.” No comma between subject and verb. Quick check: \"The houses in the historic district, are made of coral stone.\" Why is the comma wrong? … Think! … It separates the subject from the verb. Great job! Now you can say: I can write \"The new metro line that runs along the main road connects many districts.\" with no comma before the verb.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5","title":"A Proposal for Our Neighborhood: Language in Use","objectives":["Students will be able to present a proposal to improve a neighborhood.","Students will be able to write a formal proposal with quantifier relatives, -ever words, city vocabulary and correct commas, and answer SAT-style transition and synthesis items.","I can present a proposal for a better neighborhood.","I can write a formal proposal to the municipality."],"objectives_ar":["سيتمكن الطلاب من عرض مقترح لتحسين حيّ سكني.","سيتمكن الطلاب من كتابة مقترح رسمي بجمل الوصل الكمية وكلمات -ever ومفردات المدينة والفواصل الصحيحة، والإجابة عن أسئلة الروابط والتركيب بنمط SAT.","أستطيع أن أعرض مقترحًا لحيّ أفضل.","أستطيع أن أكتب مقترحًا رسميًّا إلى البلدية."],"vocabulary":[{"word":"infrastructure","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɪnfrəˈstrʌktʃɚ/","definition":"the basic systems a city needs, such as roads, water, power and transportation","arabic":"البنية التحتية","example":"The new metro is a major investment in the city's infrastructure.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"urban","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈɝbən/","definition":"connected with towns and cities","arabic":"حضري","example":"Urban areas often have problems with traffic.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"density","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈdɛnsəti/","definition":"the number of people or buildings in a particular area","arabic":"كثافة","example":"Population density is highest in the city center.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"congestion","pos":"noun","ipa":"/kənˈdʒɛstʃən/","definition":"a situation in which roads are too full of traffic","arabic":"ازدحام","example":"Traffic congestion wastes millions of hours every year.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"sustainable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/səˈsteɪnəbəl/","definition":"able to continue for a long time without harming the environment","arabic":"مستدام","example":"The city needs sustainable transportation.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"pedestrian","pos":"noun","ipa":"/pəˈdɛstriən/","definition":"a person who is walking, not driving","arabic":"المشاة / الراجل","example":"The street is now closed to cars and open only to pedestrians.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"residential","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌrɛzɪˈdɛnʃəl/","definition":"used for homes, not offices or factories","arabic":"سكني","example":"They live in a quiet residential area.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"commute","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kəˈmjut/","definition":"to travel regularly between home and work or school","arabic":"يتنقّل يوميًا","example":"Many people commute more than an hour each way.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"zoning","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈzoʊnɪŋ/","definition":"rules that say how land in each area of a city can be used","arabic":"تقسيم المناطق / التنظيم العمراني","example":"Zoning laws keep factories away from homes.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"renovate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈrɛnəˌveɪt/","definition":"to repair an old building and make it look new","arabic":"يرمّم / يجدّد","example":"The council renovated the old market.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"heritage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛrətədʒ/","definition":"buildings, traditions and culture passed down from the past","arabic":"تراث","example":"Al-Balad is part of Jeddah's heritage.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"amenity","pos":"noun","ipa":"/əˈmɛnəti/","definition":"a useful facility such as a park, shop, clinic or library","arabic":"مرفق / خدمة","example":"The neighborhood has good amenities.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"sprawl","pos":"noun","ipa":"/sprɔl/","definition":"the spread of a city into the land around it in an unplanned way","arabic":"التمدّد العمراني العشوائي","example":"Urban sprawl makes people depend on cars.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"inclusive","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˌɪnˈklusɪv/","definition":"welcoming to all people, including people with disabilities","arabic":"شامل / دامج","example":"Parks should be inclusive spaces for everyone.","image_id":"","audio_id":""},{"word":"municipality","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌmjunɪsəˈpæləti/","definition":"a city or town with its own local government","arabic":"بلدية","example":"The municipality is building new sidewalks.","image_id":"","audio_id":""}],"explanation":{"text":"Today you use all of Unit 3: quantifier relative clauses, -ever words, city vocabulary and word families, and avoiding unnecessary commas.\nA formal proposal describes the problem with facts, proposes specific solutions, explains the benefits and ends politely. Transitions guide the reader: however (contrast), in addition / also (addition), as a result / therefore (result), for example (illustration).\n✔ , most of whom… • , both of which… • , neither of which…\n✔ Whoever… • wherever… • However + adjective + subject + verb\n✔ congestion, amenities, pedestrians, green space, residential\n✔ no comma between subject and verb, or after such as","examples":["The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children.","There are two crossings, neither of which has traffic lights.","Whoever uses the park will benefit.","However small the budget is, the changes are worth it.","The proposal includes amenities such as benches and a playground.","The residents of our street want a safer place for their children.","As a result, more families would walk instead of driving.","In addition, the bike lane would reduce congestion."],"common_errors":["✗ The street has 300 families, many of them have young children. → ✓ The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children. (Avoid a comma splice.)","✗ However the budget is small, the changes are worth it. → ✓ However small the budget is, the changes are worth it. (However + adjective first.)","✗ The residents of our street, want a park. → ✓ The residents of our street want a park. (No comma before the verb.)"],"arabic":"نراجع جمل الوصل الكمية وكلمات -ever وHowever + صفة ومفردات المدينة وتجنب الفواصل غير الضرورية.\nالمقترح الرسمي يصف المشكلة بالحقائق، ويقترح حلولًا محددة، ويشرح الفوائد، وينتهي بأدب.\nالروابط: however للمقابلة، وin addition للإضافة، وas a result للنتيجة، وfor example للتمثيل."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:20","narration":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Proposal for Our Neighborhood: Language in Use”. Let’s learn quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together.","on_screen":"GV-L10-U3-LS5 | A Proposal for Our Neighborhood: Language in Use","visual":"Zaki the Falcon flies in over the Leaf Green title card and lands next to the lesson title; soft chime."},{"time":"00:20–00:45","narration":"Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Neighborhood makeover: in groups, choose a real problem in your area (no park, dangerous crossings, no shade, too much traffic). Prepare a 2-minute proposal for the municipality with one quantifier relative clause, one -ever word, one \"however + adjective\" clause and four city words. Other groups vote for the best proposal.","on_screen":"Language in use","visual":"Split screen: two cast members talking, with speech bubbles appearing."},{"time":"00:45–01:30","narration":"Today you use all of Unit 3: quantifier relative clauses, -ever words, city vocabulary and word families, and avoiding unnecessary commas. A formal proposal describes the problem with facts, proposes specific solutions, explains the benefits and ends politely. Transitions guide the reader: however (contrast), in addition / also (addition), as a result / therefore (result), for example (illustration).","on_screen":"✔ , most of whom… • , both of which… • , neither of which… • ✔ Whoever… • wherever… • However + adjective + subject + verb • ✔ congestion, amenities, pedestrians, green space, residential","visual":"Grammar box builds line by line (form → meaning → use); Zaki points to each line."},{"time":"01:30–02:15","narration":"Here are some examples. The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children. There are two crossings, neither of which has traffic lights. Whoever uses the park will benefit.","on_screen":"The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children. / There are two crossings, neither of which has traffic lights. / Whoever uses the park will benefit.","visual":"Each example appears on a card with a small picture; the target part bounces once and turns green."},{"time":"02:15–02:45","narration":"Be careful! Some learners say: “The street has 300 families, many of them have young children.” That’s not right. We say: “The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children.” Avoid a comma splice.","on_screen":"✗ The street has 300 families, many of them have young children.   ✓ The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children.","visual":"A red cross stamps the wrong sentence; it slides away and the correct sentence appears with a green tick."},{"time":"02:45–03:15","narration":"Quick check: what is wrong with “However the budget is small, the changes are worth it.”? … Think! … It should be “However small the budget is, the changes are worth it.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a proposal for a better neighborhood.","on_screen":"Quick check ✔  |  I can: I can present a proposal for a better neighborhood.","visual":"Three-second pause with a countdown ring; Zaki gives a wing \"thumbs up\" and confetti in the programme colours."}],"captions_ar":["مرحبًا، أنا زكي! درس اليوم: quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review).","استمع وانتبه إلى الكلمات الملوّنة بالأخضر.","نراجع جمل الوصل الكمية وكلمات -ever وHowever + صفة ومفردات المدينة وتجنب الفواصل غير الضرورية.","لاحظ الأمثلة: الجزء الملوّن هو القاعدة.","انتبه! الخطأ: The street has 300 families, many of them have young children. — الصحيح: The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children.","سؤال سريع… فكّر ثم أجب. أحسنت! الآن تستطيع: أستطيع أن أعرض مقترحًا لحيّ أفضل."]},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad","body":"Al-Rawdah Street is home to about 300 families, many of whom have young children.\nHowever, the street has no park and only two crossings, neither of which has traffic lights.\nAt rush hour, congestion makes it dangerous for pedestrians to cross.\nWe propose turning the empty land near the mosque into a small green space with amenities such as shaded benches, a playground and a walking path.\nWe also suggest adding a raised crossing and a bike lane, both of which would make the street safer.\nHowever small the budget is, these changes would improve residents' quality of life.\nWhoever uses the new park, from children to grandparents, will benefit.\nWe hope the municipality will consider our proposal.","word_count":118,"audio_id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS5-T1"}],"activities":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-A1","type":"true_false","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Ziyad's proposal. True or False?","items":[{"q":"About 300 families live on Al-Rawdah Street.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"The street already has a park near the mosque.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Both crossings have traffic lights.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Ziyad suggests a playground and a walking path.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"He also proposes a bike lane and a raised crossing.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"True","explanation":"Check the text “A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."},{"q":"Ziyad says the changes will only help children.","options":["True","False"],"answer":"False","explanation":"Check the text “A Proposal for Al-Rawdah Street – Ziyad”.","explanation_ar":"ارجع إلى النص وابحث عن الجملة التي تعطي الإجابة."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-B1","type":"mcq","instructions":"Guided practice: Choose the correct answer.","items":[{"q":"The street is home to 300 families, many of ___ have young children.","options":["whom","them","who","which"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"Quantifier relative clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل الكمية."},{"q":"There are two crossings, neither of which ___ traffic lights.","options":["have","are having","having","has"],"answer":"has","explanation":"Quantifier relative clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل الكمية."},{"q":"___ uses the new park will benefit.","options":["Wherever","Whoever","Whichever","Whatever"],"answer":"Whoever","explanation":"-ever word or however + adjective.","explanation_ar":"كلمات -ever."},{"q":"At rush hour, ___ makes it dangerous for pedestrians to cross.","options":["heritage","density","zoning","congestion"],"answer":"congestion","explanation":"City vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المدينة."},{"q":"","options":["The proposal includes amenities such as shaded benches and a playground.","The proposal, includes amenities such as shaded benches and a playground.","The proposal includes amenities such as, shaded benches and a playground.","The proposal includes, amenities such as shaded benches and a playground."],"answer":"The proposal includes amenities such as shaded benches and a playground.","explanation":"Unnecessary comma.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"Al-Rawdah Street has no park and only two crossings. ______ children have nowhere safe to play near their homes.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For example,","However,","As a result,","In contrast,"],"answer":"As a result,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-C1","type":"open","instructions":"Freer practice: Prepare your proposal.","items":[{"q":"The problem in your neighborhood: ___","options":[],"answer":"Our street has no shade, so it is too hot to walk in the afternoon.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A fact with a quantifier relative clause: ___","options":[],"answer":"About 200 students walk to school every day, most of whom cross the main road.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Your solution with \"such as\": ___","options":[],"answer":"We propose planting trees and adding amenities such as covered bus stops.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A sentence with \"However + adjective…\": ___","options":[],"answer":"However hot the weather is, students would be able to walk in the shade.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"A polite ending: ___","options":[],"answer":"We would be grateful if the municipality could consider our proposal.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-D1","type":"open","instructions":"Production: Write your proposal.","items":[{"q":"Plan: problem, facts, solutions, benefits, polite ending.","options":[],"answer":"Problem: no safe crossing near school; facts: 200 students; solutions: traffic lights, speed bumps, shaded path; benefits: safety, less congestion; ending: request a meeting","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Write your neighborhood proposal to the municipality (8–9 sentences).","options":[],"answer":"Our neighborhood has two schools, both of which are on the main road. About 600 students attend them, most of whom walk to school every morning. However, the road has no traffic lights, and congestion at rush hour makes crossing dangerous. We propose adding a pedestrian crossing with traffic lights near each school. We also suggest planting trees along the sidewalk and adding amenities such as covered benches. However limited the budget is, safety must come first. In addition, these changes would encourage more families to walk instead of driving. Whoever uses the road, whether drivers or pedestrians, will benefit. We hope the municipality will consider our proposal.","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."},{"q":"Check: \"of whom / of which\" (not \"of them\")? \"However + adjective\"? no comma before the verb or after \"such as\"? Fix one mistake.","options":[],"answer":"I changed \"About 600 students attend them, most of them walk to school\" to \"most of whom walk to school\".","explanation":"Answers will vary. Accept any answer that uses quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) correctly and makes sense.","explanation_ar":"تختلف الإجابات. تُقبل أي إجابة صحيحة لغويًا تستخدم قاعدة الدرس ومعناها واضح."}]}],"game":{"name":"Board Race Review","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["One student from each team stands at the board.","The teacher says a cue word and a task (meaning, spelling or sentence with quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review)).","The first correct answer wins a point; rotate players every round.","Play 10 rounds."],"data":[{"cue":"infrastructure","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"urban","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"density","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"congestion","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"sustainable","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"pedestrian","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"residential","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"commute","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"zoning","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"renovate","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"heritage","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"amenity","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"sprawl","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"inclusive","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]},{"cue":"municipality","tasks":["meaning","spelling","sentence"]}]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Our school has three gates, only one of ___ has a crossing.","options":["it","which","them","whom"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Quantifier relative clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل الكمية."},{"q":"The council invited the residents, most of ___ attended the meeting.","options":["which","who","them","whom"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"Quantifier relative clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل الكمية."},{"q":"___ late you arrive, the metro will still be running.","options":["However","Whatever","Whichever","Whoever"],"answer":"However","explanation":"-ever word or however + adjective.","explanation_ar":"كلمات -ever."},{"q":"Young families in the city need more ___ housing.","options":["affordable","affordably","affording","afford"],"answer":"affordable","explanation":"City vocabulary.","explanation_ar":"مفردات المدينة."},{"q":"","options":["The children who live, on our street play in the new park every day.","The children who live on our street, play in the new park every day.","The children who live on our street play in the new park every day.","The children, who live on our street play in the new park every day."],"answer":"The children who live on our street play in the new park every day.","explanation":"Unnecessary comma.","explanation_ar":"الفاصلة غير الضرورية."},{"q":"Many residents expected the new metro to be empty. ______ more than half a million trips were made in the first weeks.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["Similarly,","Instead,","Therefore,","For example,"],"answer":"Instead,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"The old market was renovated in 2023. ______ the number of visitors has doubled.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["However,","Before that,","Since then,","In contrast,"],"answer":"Since then,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Al-Balad is the historic district of Jeddah.\n• It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2014.\n• Many of its coral-stone houses are several centuries old.\n• A large restoration project is protecting the buildings.\n\nThe student wants to explain why Al-Balad is being restored. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Al-Balad became a UNESCO site in 2014.","Al-Balad is in Jeddah.","Jeddah has many districts.","Because Al-Balad, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, contains coral-stone houses that are several centuries old, a large project is restoring its buildings."],"answer":"Because Al-Balad, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, contains coral-stone houses that are several centuries old, a large project is restoring its buildings.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Urban sprawl means a city spreads into the land around it.\n• Sprawling cities often lack public transportation.\n• Residents of such cities depend on cars.\n• This leads to traffic congestion and pollution.\n\nThe student wants to explain a negative effect of urban sprawl. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Many people own cars.","Because sprawling cities often lack public transportation, their residents depend on cars, which leads to congestion and pollution.","Some cities have public transportation.","Urban sprawl means a city spreads into the land around it."],"answer":"Because sprawling cities often lack public transportation, their residents depend on cars, which leads to congestion and pollution.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"Pick the best opening sentence for Ziyad's proposal to the municipality:","options":["Our street is home to 300 families, many of who have young children.","Our street is home to 300 families, many of them have young children.","Our street, is home to 300 families many of whom have young children.","Our street is home to 300 families, many of whom have young children."],"answer":"Our street is home to 300 families, many of whom have young children.","explanation":"Quantifier relative clause.","explanation_ar":"جملة الوصل الكمية."}],"images":[{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-IMG01","prompt":"A neighborhood council meeting in a community hall: Ziyad presenting a map of his street with a proposed park, crosswalks and a bike lane; residents of different ages listening; a municipal officer taking notes. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Landscape 16:9.","alt":"A neighborhood council meeting in a community hall: Ziyad presenting a map of his street with a proposed park, crosswalks and a bike lane; residents of different ages listening; a municipal officer taking notes"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-IMG02","prompt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Cities and Communities”, speech bubbles left empty. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image; characters drawn as older teens/young adults aged 16–19, editorial infographic feel. Square 1:1.","alt":"two programme characters doing the speaking task about “Cities and Communities”, speech bubbles left empty"},{"id":"GV-L10-U3-LS5-G01","prompt":"Card-back and board design for the classroom game “Board Race Review”: Zaki the Falcon icon in the centre, Leaf Green border with navy stars. Clean flat vector illustration, soft rounded outlines (2 px navy #2E3176), gentle flat shading, programme palette: navy #2E3176, coral #E06377, sun gold #F0B232, sky blue #2A90C5, leaf green #3FA66B accent; white or very light background; modest, culturally appropriate clothing for Saudi schools; boys and girls shown in separate groups in school scenes; no text, letters or logos inside the image. Portrait 2:3.","alt":"Game card design for Board Race Review"}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS5-T1","script":"Al-Rawdah Street is home to about 300 families, many of whom have young children. However, the street has no park and only two crossings, neither of which has traffic lights. At rush hour, congestion makes it dangerous for pedestrians to cross. We propose turning the empty land near the mosque into a small green space with amenities such as shaded benches, a playground and a walking path. We also suggest adding a raised crossing and a bike lane, both of which would make the street safer. However small the budget is, these changes would improve residents' quality of life. Whoever uses the new park, from children to grandparents, will benefit. We hope the municipality will consider our proposal.","voice":"en-US, articulate adult voice","speed":"1.05"},{"id":"AUD-GV-L10-U3-LS5-VID","script":"Hi, I’m Zaki! Today’s lesson is “A Proposal for Our Neighborhood: Language in Use”. Let’s learn quantifier relative clauses, -ever words and however + adjective, urban planning vocabulary and word families, avoiding unnecessary commas; SAT transitions and rhetorical synthesis (unit review) together. Let’s use everything from this unit. Your task: Neighborhood makeover: in groups, choose a real problem in your area (no park, dangerous crossings, no shade, too much traffic). Prepare a 2-minute proposal for the municipality with one quantifier relative clause, one -ever word, one \"however + adjective\" clause and four city words. Other groups vote for the best proposal. Today you use all of Unit 3: quantifier relative clauses, -ever words, city vocabulary and word families, and avoiding unnecessary commas. A formal proposal describes the problem with facts, proposes specific solutions, explains the benefits and ends politely. Transitions guide the reader: however (contrast), in addition / also (addition), as a result / therefore (result), for example (illustration). Here are some examples. The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children. There are two crossings, neither of which has traffic lights. Whoever uses the park will benefit. Be careful! Some learners say: “The street has 300 families, many of them have young children.” That’s not right. We say: “The street has 300 families, many of whom have young children.” Avoid a comma splice. Quick check: what is wrong with “However the budget is small, the changes are worth it.”? … Think! … It should be “However small the budget is, the changes are worth it.”. Great job! Now you can say: I can present a proposal for a better neighborhood.","voice":"en-US, Zaki the Falcon narrator voice (bright, friendly)","speed":"1.05"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"The city has millions of residents, many of ___ use the metro.","options":["whom","which","who","them"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"quantifier + of whom (people).","explanation_ar":"of whom للأشخاص."},{"q":"The council built four parks, all of ___ have playgrounds.","options":["whom","which","that","them"],"answer":"which","explanation":"quantifier + of which (things).","explanation_ar":"of which للأشياء."},{"q":"The library has two reading rooms, neither of which ___ open on Fridays.","options":["is","were","are","have been"],"answer":"is","explanation":"Agreement after each / all / neither of which.","explanation_ar":"الاتفاق."},{"q":"The mosques, the oldest of ___ dates from the Ottoman period, are all being restored.","options":["whom","them","it","which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"the + superlative + of which / of whom.","explanation_ar":"the oldest of which."},{"q":"The Antarctic ice sheet, the thickness ______ exceeds four kilometers in places, contains most of the fresh water on Earth.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["which","of which","of it","of whom"],"answer":"of which","explanation":"noun + of which (= whose).","explanation_ar":"the walls of which."},{"q":"The study followed 500 commuters, ______ reported that the metro had reduced their travel time.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["most of who","most of them","most of which","most of whom"],"answer":"most of whom","explanation":"of whom / of which, not of them (comma splice).","explanation_ar":"تجنب of them."},{"q":"___ cleans the classroom last must turn off the lights.","options":["Whatever","Whichever","Wherever","Whoever"],"answer":"Whoever","explanation":"whoever = any person who.","explanation_ar":"whoever."},{"q":"You can use ___ entrance is closer to your office.","options":["whoever","however","whichever","wherever"],"answer":"whichever","explanation":"whichever = any one of a limited choice.","explanation_ar":"whichever."},{"q":"___ tired I am, I always walk in the evening.","options":["However","Whatever","Whichever","Whenever"],"answer":"However","explanation":"However + adjective + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"However + صفة."},{"q":"","options":["The museum welcomes whatever wants to learn about local history.","The museum welcomes wherever wants to learn about local history.","The museum welcomes whomever wants to learn about local history.","The museum welcomes whoever wants to learn about local history."],"answer":"The museum welcomes whoever wants to learn about local history.","explanation":"whoever (subject) vs whomever (object).","explanation_ar":"whoever أم whomever."},{"q":"___ where you live, you deserve clean air.","options":["Whoever","However","No matter","Whatever"],"answer":"No matter","explanation":"no matter who / what / where / how.","explanation_ar":"no matter."},{"q":"Traffic ___ is the biggest problem in many large cities.","options":["congested","congest","congesting","congestion"],"answer":"congestion","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The district is mainly ___, with very few shops or offices.","options":["residential","reside","resident","residence"],"answer":"residential","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."},{"q":"The government is investing billions in roads, water pipes and power lines to improve the country's ______.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","options":["infrastructure","zoning","sprawl","heritage"],"answer":"infrastructure","explanation":"Words in context (SAT style).","explanation_ar":"الكلمات في السياق."},{"q":"The new rules about which areas can have factories are called ___ laws.","options":["heritage","zoning","sprawl","amenity"],"answer":"zoning","explanation":"City word with the right meaning.","explanation_ar":"المعنى."},{"q":"","options":["The narrow streets, in the old part of town are closed to cars.","The narrow streets in the old part, of town are closed to cars.","The narrow streets in the old part of town are closed to cars.","The narrow streets in the old part of town, are closed to cars."],"answer":"The narrow streets in the old part of town are closed to cars.","explanation":"No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"","options":["Engineers discovered that, the bridge needed urgent repairs.","Engineers discovered, that the bridge needed urgent repairs.","Engineers discovered that the bridge needed urgent repairs.","Engineers, discovered that the bridge needed urgent repairs."],"answer":"Engineers discovered that the bridge needed urgent repairs.","explanation":"No comma between a verb and its object or that-clause.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفعل ومفعوله."},{"q":"","options":["Taif is famous for products such as, roses, honey and pomegranates.","Taif is famous, for products such as roses, honey and pomegranates.","Taif is famous for products, such as, roses, honey and pomegranates.","Taif is famous for products such as roses, honey and pomegranates."],"answer":"Taif is famous for products such as roses, honey and pomegranates.","explanation":"No comma after such as / including.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بعد such as."},{"q":"Researchers who study traffic patterns in large ______ that adding new lanes to highways often leads to even more cars within a few years.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","options":["cities, have found,","cities have found","cities have found,","cities, have found"],"answer":"cities have found","explanation":"No comma between subject and verb.","explanation_ar":"لا فاصلة بين الفاعل والفعل."},{"q":"Planners once believed that wider roads would end traffic jams. ______ wider roads often attract more drivers and make congestion worse.\n\nWhich choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","options":["For this reason,","In fact,","Similarly,","Therefore,"],"answer":"In fact,","explanation":"Logical transition.","explanation_ar":"الرابط المنطقي."},{"q":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n• Mixed-use developments combine homes, shops and offices.\n• Residents can walk to work and to the shops.\n• This reduces the need for cars.\n• Several new districts in Saudi cities follow this model.\n\nThe student wants to explain an advantage of mixed-use development. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","options":["Mixed-use developments are new.","Several districts are in Saudi cities.","Because mixed-use developments combine homes, shops and offices, residents can walk to work and to the shops, which reduces the need for cars.","Many people have cars."],"answer":"Because mixed-use developments combine homes, shops and offices, residents can walk to work and to the shops, which reduces the need for cars.","explanation":"Rhetorical synthesis.","explanation_ar":"التركيب البلاغي."},{"q":"How can we fix it? \"the residents of our street, want a park, most of them have children.\"","options":["The residents of our street want a park, most of them have children.","The residents of our street want a park; most of them have children.","The residents of our street, want a park; most of them have children.","the residents of our street want a park; most of them have children."],"answer":"The residents of our street want a park; most of them have children.","explanation":"of whom / of which, not of them (comma splice).","explanation_ar":"تجنب of them."},{"q":"\"However busy the streets are, the bus lane stays clear.\" What does \"however\" mean here?","options":["no matter how","but","for example","therefore"],"answer":"no matter how","explanation":"However + adjective + subject + verb.","explanation_ar":"However + صفة."},{"q":"Which word family is correct?","options":["urban → urbanity → urbanly","urban → urbanation → urbanizing","urban → urbanize → urbanization","urbanize → urbanist → urbanous"],"answer":"urban → urbanize → urbanization","explanation":"Word-family form (noun, verb, adjective, adverb).","explanation_ar":"عائلة الكلمة."}],"theme_ar":"المدن والمجتمعات","essential_question_ar":"ما الذي يجعل المدينة مكانًا جيدًا للعيش؟","can_do":"I can add information with quantifier relative clauses (most of whom, all of which), use whoever, whatever and wherever for general reference and concession, discuss urban planning with word families, and avoid unnecessary commas.","can_do_ar":"أستطيع أن أضيف معلومات بجمل وصل كمية (most of whom / all of which)، وأستخدم whoever وwhatever وwherever للتعميم والتنازل، وأناقش التخطيط العمراني بعائلات الكلمات، وأتجنب الفواصل غير الضرورية."});
